407 0 obj
<<
/Title (As AI moves from tool to active collaborator in how decisions are made, the conditions governing that collaboration become consequential at an organisational level. The failure runs in both directions. Organisations where AI is used without governance create risk that outpaces the value being generated: liability exposure, accountability gaps, inconsistency that erodes rather than builds confidence. Organisations where governance was designed primarily to prevent risk rather than enable use create a different and equally costly problem. Capable people work carefully around AI rather than genuinely with it, generating the cost of adoption without its value. Neither is a stable position. The organisations producing durable return have built something more specific than a policy: the conditions in which people can trust their own judgement about when and how to use AI, and the organisation can trust theirs.)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 397 0 R
/Prev 418 0 R
/First 419 0 R
/Last 419 0 R
/Count -1
>>
endobj
408 0 obj
<<
/Title (The questions that dominate board-level AI conversations are reasonable ones. Are we moving at the right pace? Is our governance adequate? How do we build the talent pipeline? How do we manage the risk? They are not wrong questions. For most organisations asking them, they are arriving in the wrong order.)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 391 0 R
/Prev 398 0 R
/Next 420 0 R
>>
endobj
409 0 obj
<<
/Title (The prior question, the one that should precede every significant AI investment decision, every board conversation on digital maturity, every engagement with a transformation partner, is this: which of the six conditions is most likely our binding constraint? A measured, benchmarked answer precise enough to form a sequencing hypothesis, in what order to invest, to what end.)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 391 0 R
