>>
endobj
415 0 obj
<<
/Title (This pattern repeats across sectors, organisation types, and geographies with a consistency that rules out programme design as the explanation. What the upskilling initiative could not address, what no training programme is built to address, is the question of identity. The people in that programme had spent ten, twenty, or thirty years building expertise, judgment, and professional craft in a particular way. That expertise is real and valued. It is also built on a foundation that AI now partially displaces. The skills transfer asks people to redesign not just how they work but what their competence means. That is not a learning challenge. It is a conditions challenge. It requires psychological safety to experiment without the risk of looking incompetent in front of colleagues and clients. It requires an organisational environment where identity is not threatened by change but supported through it. Skills without those conditions do not hold. They are acquired in a programme and quietly abandoned when the programme ends, because the deeper conditions for sustained different behaviour were never established.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 404 0 R
/Prev 424 0 R
>>
endobj
416 0 obj
<<
/Title (THE MISSING MIDDLE)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 405 0 R
/Next 425 0 R
>>
endobj
417 0 obj
<<
/Title (for delivery, for team performance, for operational continuity. AI transformation arrives as an additional ask on an already full accountability set. No adjusted metric, no additional resource, no clear signal about how it should be prioritised against everything else they are measured on. They are not opposed to the strategy. They are accountable for other things. The strategy exists. It never moves. The cause is structural, and the missing middle is a consistent bottleneck in AI transformation at scale.)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 405 0 R
/Prev 426 0 R
>>
endobj
418 0 obj
<<
/Title (A senior manager needs to make a significant decision. The AI system available to her has produced an output that is more thorough and better structured than anything she could have produced in the time available. Her organisation has an AI policy. It states that AI tools may be used "where appropriate and with professional judgement." It does not define appropriate. It does not tell her whether she is accountable for the output if she uses it, how to disclose it, or whether a decision made with AI input carries different weight than one made without it. She sets the output aside and makes the decision the way she has always made it. Two hours later than she needed to. The value was available. The conditions for using it were not.)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 397 0 R
/Prev 406 0 R
/Next 407 0 R
>>
